---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { PROJECTS } from '../../data/site';
export function getStaticPaths() { return PROJECTS.map(project=>({params:{slug:project.slug},props:{project}})); }
const { project } = Astro.props;
const topics = {
  'ecommerce-clv-churn':'Customer segments and churn intelligence',
  'hr-attrition-analysis':'Confidential employee attrition explorer',
  'ab-test':'A/B testing calculator',
  'sql-for-business-analytics':'SQL query and cleaning workbench',
  'co2-emissions':'CO₂ country and year explorer',
  'analytics-agent':'Analytics-agent demonstration',
  'ai-tell':'Document inspection walkthrough',
  'analytics-projects':'Dashboard collection'
};
---
<Base title={`${topics[project.slug]} | Project lab`} description={`Interactive supporting material for ${project.name}`}>
 <main class="lab-shell" data-lab={project.slug}>
   <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/projects/">Projects</a><span aria-hidden="true"> / </span><a href={`/projects/${project.slug}/`}>{project.name}</a><span aria-hidden="true"> / </span>Interactive lab</nav>
   <header class="lab-header"><p class="eyebrow">PROJECT FIELD NOTES / INTERACTIVE LAB</p><h1>{topics[project.slug]}</h1><p class="intro">Explore the supporting evidence, assumptions and methods. Real project outputs and illustrative examples are identified separately.</p><div class="lab-links"><a href={`/projects/${project.slug}/`}>Read the original case study</a><a href={project.repo} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Inspect source repository</a></div></header>
   {project.slug==='ecommerce-clv-churn' && <section aria-labelledby="customer-heading"><h2 id="customer-heading">Explore historical RFM segments</h2><p class="data-note">Verified aggregate data: 5,720 named customers. These are historical customer and revenue shares, not customer-level churn probabilities or predicted CLV. The model performance comparison uses a separate 4,311-customer evaluation cohort.</p><div class="controls"><label for="segment-filter">Segment<select id="segment-filter"><option value="all">All segments</option></select></label><label for="segment-measure">Compare<select id="segment-measure"><option value="revenue_pct">Historical revenue share (%)</option><option value="customer_pct">Customer share (%)</option><option value="avg_recency">Average days since purchase</option><option value="avg_frequency">Average purchase frequency</option></select></label></div><div id="customer-summary" class="stat-grid" aria-live="polite"></div><div id="customer-chart" class="bars" role="img" aria-label="Customer segment comparison"></div><h3>Churn risk interpretation</h3><p>RFM recency suggests which groups merit investigation, but it is not a calibrated individual churn score. The corrected churn classifier reaches 0.796 ROC-AUC (Random Forest) while Logistic Regression has recall 0.830 in the published comparison. Do not combine these model evaluation results with the segment totals as though they describe the same cohort.</p><h3>Walkthrough</h3><ol><li>Select a segment to inspect historical behavior and revenue share.</li><li>Switch the measure to compare the size and value concentration.</li><li>Use the high-value win-back export in the original project to plan a candidate outreach list, subject to privacy and eligibility review.</li><li>Test campaign lift with a holdout group before claiming retained revenue.</li></ol><p><a href="/data/ecommerce/segment-summary.csv" download>Download verified segment aggregates (CSV)</a> · <a href="/data/ecommerce/model-comparison.csv" download>Download verified model scores (CSV)</a></p></section>}
   {project.slug==='hr-attrition-analysis' && <section aria-labelledby="hr-heading"><h2 id="hr-heading">Explore the reported employee groups</h2><p class="data-note">Organization: <strong>Confidential</strong>. This demo uses only published, aggregated case-study counts. It does not expose individual records or the withheld assessment. The group categories overlap and must not be summed.</p><div class="controls"><label for="hr-group">Comparison<select id="hr-group"><option value="workload">Overtime vs no overtime</option><option value="roles">Sales Representatives vs overall</option><option value="experience">Under 5 years experience vs overall</option></select></label></div><div id="hr-summary" class="stat-grid" aria-live="polite"></div><div id="hr-chart" class="bars" role="img" aria-label="Attrition comparison"></div><h3>Reading the findings</h3><p>Overall, 237 of 1,470 records were marked as leavers (16.1%). The overtime group recorded 127 of 416 (30.5%), compared with 110 of 1,054 (10.4%) without overtime. Sales Representatives recorded 33 of 83 (39.8%). Less than five years of total working experience recorded 75 of 228 (32.9%). These proportions are descriptive, not causal or annualized rates.</p><p>The original Power BI screenshots and DAX logic remain in the <a href="/projects/hr-attrition-analysis/">case study</a>. An embeddable public Power BI report is not provided, so this accessible companion summarizes verified aggregates instead.</p></section>}
   {project.slug==='ab-test' && <section aria-labelledby="ab-heading"><h2 id="ab-heading">Plan and evaluate a two-arm conversion experiment</h2><p class="data-note">Calculator uses a normal approximation, equal allocation, two-sided 95% confidence intervals and a two-sided z-test. Planning uses a normal-approximation sample-size formula with 80% power by default. Small counts require exact methods or additional statistical review. All input defaults are illustrative, not extracted experiment results.</p><div class="controls"><label for="ab-baseline">Baseline conversion (%)<input id="ab-baseline" type="number" min=".1" max="99" step=".1" value="10"></label><label for="ab-mde">Minimum detectable absolute lift (percentage points)<input id="ab-mde" type="number" min=".1" max="90" step=".1" value="2"></label><label for="ab-power">Desired power<select id="ab-power"><option value="0.8">80%</option><option value="0.9">90%</option></select></label></div><p id="ab-planning" class="output" aria-live="polite"></p><h3>Observed outcomes</h3><div class="controls"><label for="ab-na">Control visitors<input id="ab-na" type="number" min="1" step="1" value="1000"></label><label for="ab-ca">Control conversions<input id="ab-ca" type="number" min="0" step="1" value="100"></label><label for="ab-nb">Variant visitors<input id="ab-nb" type="number" min="1" step="1" value="1000"></label><label for="ab-cb">Variant conversions<input id="ab-cb" type="number" min="0" step="1" value="125"></label></div><div id="ab-result" class="stat-grid" aria-live="polite"></div><p id="ab-interpretation" class="output" aria-live="polite"></p><details><summary>Worked example and calculations</summary><p>Illustrative example: A has 100 conversions from 1,000 visits (10.0%), B has 125 from 1,000 (12.5%). Absolute difference B minus A = 2.5 percentage points. The interval uses the unpooled Wald standard error: sqrt[pA(1-pA)/nA + pB(1-pB)/nB]; CI = difference ± 1.96 × SE. A pooled standard error is used for the two-proportion z-test. Statistical significance is not proof of business value.</p><p>Sample size per arm uses 2 × (z(1 − α/2) + z(power))² × p̄(1 − p̄) / MDE², with p̄ the average of assumed baseline and target conversion. This is a planning approximation rather than a guarantee.</p></details></section>}
   {project.slug==='sql-for-business-analytics' && <section aria-labelledby="sql-heading"><h2 id="sql-heading">Runnable SQL mini workbench</h2><p class="data-note">Illustrative, synthetic orders. The source project demonstrates Chinook joins, COVID analysis and Nashville cleaning, but those original large datasets are not reproduced here. SQLite runs locally in the browser using a downloadable WebAssembly library; no queries or customer records leave the page.</p><div class="controls"><label for="sql-example">Example query<select id="sql-example"><option value="totals">Revenue by customer</option><option value="rank">Rank orders by value</option><option value="clean">Normalize city and de-duplicate</option></select></label></div><label for="sql-editor">Edit SQL<textarea id="sql-editor" rows="9" spellcheck="false" aria-describedby="sql-help"></textarea></label><p id="sql-help" class="fine">The sample database contains six made-up orders. Press Run query to execute SQLite. Browser must permit access to the external sql.js runtime.</p><button id="sql-run" type="button">Run query</button><p id="sql-status" role="status"></p><div id="sql-output" class="table-wrap" aria-live="polite"></div><details><summary>Before and after cleaning</summary><p>Before: city values include “ cairo ” and “CAIRO”; order 103 occurs twice. After: city casing/spacing is normalized and duplicates are removed using ROW_NUMBER() over order_id. This is an illustrative cleaning pattern, not a claim about the Nashville data.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Illustrative records</caption><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Order ID</th><th>City</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Before</td><td>103, 103</td><td> cairo , CAIRO</td></tr><tr><td>After</td><td>103</td><td>Cairo</td></tr></tbody></table></div></details><p><a href="https://github.com/MMS-21/SQL-for-Business-Analytics/blob/main/NashvilleHousing%20-Data%20Cleaning%20Project.sql">Inspect original Nashville cleaning SQL</a></p></section>}
   {project.slug==='co2-emissions' && <section aria-labelledby="co2-heading"><h2 id="co2-heading">Country emissions over time</h2><p class="data-note">Verified annual national CO₂ emissions from the CSV archived in the original project, filtered to 20 illustrative comparison countries and 2000–2020. Values are tonnes of CO₂, not CO₂-equivalent or consumption-based emissions. Per-person estimates require matching population data from the World Bank API.</p><div class="controls"><label for="co2-country">Country<select id="co2-country"></select></label><label for="co2-year">Year <output id="co2-year-label">2020</output><input id="co2-year" type="range" min="2000" max="2020" step="1" value="2020"></label><label for="co2-mode">Measure<select id="co2-mode"><option value="total">Total emissions (million tonnes)</option><option value="capita">Emissions per person (tonnes/person)</option></select></label></div><p id="co2-summary" class="output" role="status"></p><div id="co2-chart" class="chart-surface" role="img" aria-label="CO₂ emissions trend from 2000 to 2020"></div><div id="co2-table" class="table-wrap"></div><h3>Source and calculation</h3><p>Total Mt = reported annual tonnes / 1,000,000. Per-person tonnes = reported annual tonnes / World Bank population for the matching country and year. If the live population feed is unavailable, no per-person value is displayed. Historical source files may be revised by their publishers.</p><p><a href="/project-labs/co2-annual-2000-2020.csv" download>Download archived total emissions sample (CSV)</a> · <a href="https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/annual-co2-emissions-per-country">Our World in Data annual emissions</a> · <a href="https://data.worldbank.org/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL">World Bank population</a></p></section>}
   {project.slug==='analytics-agent' && <section aria-labelledby="agent-heading"><h2 id="agent-heading">How a checked analysis becomes a report</h2><p class="data-note">This walkthrough is a deterministic, illustrative browser reproduction, not a live LLM run. It uses an openly available example report structure from the agent repository, without exposing private agent credentials or executing its backend.</p><label for="agent-question">Sample question<input id="agent-question" value="Which sales region has the highest revenue?" readonly></label><div class="controls"><label for="agent-step">Inspect a step<select id="agent-step"><option value="0">1. Inspect sample data</option><option value="1">2. Calculate regional totals</option><option value="2">3. Validate metrics and chart</option><option value="3">4. Report and recommendations</option></select></label></div><div id="agent-output" class="output" aria-live="polite"></div><div id="agent-chart" class="bars"></div><p><a href="/project-labs/agent-sales.csv" download>Download project synthetic sales sample (CSV)</a> · <a href="https://github.com/MMS-21/analytics-agent/blob/main/examples/churnriskbysegment.md">Inspect the project’s existing generated example report</a>. A runnable service with model credentials is not exposed on the public portfolio.</p></section>}
   {project.slug==='ai-tell' && <section aria-labelledby="aitell-heading"><h2 id="aitell-heading">Inspect a sample document</h2><p class="data-note">Illustrative document and browser-side checks only. This is not a real ai-tell backend report, authorship determination or detector accuracy result. The desktop application supports additional stylometry, provenance metadata and Unicode checks locally.</p><label for="aitell-input">Editable sample document<textarea id="aitell-input" rows="7">Project update. The team completed the first review.\nWe checked the sales figures, validated the totals, and recorded three items for follow-up.</textarea></label><button id="aitell-run" type="button">Inspect sample text</button><div id="aitell-report" class="output" role="status"></div><h3>Walkthrough</h3><ol><li>Choose a local document in the desktop app or edit the safe text sample above.</li><li>Inspect sentence and word counts, variation, invisible characters and document metadata.</li><li>Review each signal and its limitations. No text-only pattern proves whether a person or model wrote a document.</li><li>Clean metadata or compare revisions locally, then export the detailed report from the application.</li></ol><p><a href="https://github.com/MMS-21/ai-tell/releases/latest">Desktop releases and download</a> · <a href="https://github.com/MMS-21/ai-tell#features">Feature and privacy documentation</a>. A public report created by the full application was not located.</p></section>}
   {project.slug==='analytics-projects' && <section aria-labelledby="dash-heading"><h2 id="dash-heading">Dashboard collection gallery</h2><p class="data-note">The repositories include public screenshots and dashboard source files. No verified public Power BI or Tableau embed URL was found, so these are source and static-preview links, not live interactive embeds. Open a project to inspect its existing material.</p><div class="dash-grid"><article><h3>MelodyMart performance</h3><p>Explore: Which genres and cities drive music-store revenue?</p><a href="https://github.com/MMS-21/Analytics-Projects/tree/main/MelodyMart%20Performance%20Analysis">Source and screenshot</a></article><article><h3>Plant Co. Power BI</h3><p>Explore: Which countries contribute most to year-to-date sales variance?</p><a href="https://github.com/MMS-21/Analytics-Projects/tree/main/Power%20BI%20Dashboard%20Plant%20Co.%20Performance%202022%20-%20204">PBIX and screenshots</a></article><article><h3>King County housing Tableau</h3><p>Explore: How does sale price vary with house size and condition?</p><a href="https://github.com/MMS-21/Analytics-Projects/tree/main/KING%20COUNTY%2C%20WASHINGTON%20HOUSE%20SALES%20DASHBOARD">Tableau workbook and previews</a></article><article><h3>Hospital operations</h3><p>Explore: Which departments face the longest surgery wait times?</p><a href="https://github.com/MMS-21/Analytics-Projects/tree/main/Hospital%20Operational%20Efficiency">Project evidence</a></article><article><h3>Patient satisfaction</h3><p>Explore: How does satisfaction differ by department and age group?</p><a href="https://github.com/MMS-21/Analytics-Projects/tree/main/Patient%20Satisfaction%20Analysis">Project evidence</a></article><article><h3>Global CO₂ visualization</h3><p>Explore: Which countries differ most on per-capita versus total emissions?</p><a href="/project-labs/co2-emissions/">Interactive CO₂ companion</a></article></div><p>Publishing links or approved public embeds can replace these source links when they are available.</p></section>}
   <footer class="lab-footer"><a href={`/projects/${project.slug}/`}>Return to case study</a><a href="/projects/">All projects</a></footer>
 </main>
 <link rel="stylesheet" href="/project-labs/labs.css" />
 <script src="/project-labs/labs.js" defer></script>
</Base>